# Django & DRF conventions

## Settings and env

- Read config via `config/settings.py` helpers (`get_bool_env`, `get_list_env`). Add new toggles to `.env.example` with comments, not inline secrets.
- Production defaults assume HTTPS when `DEBUG=False`; local Docker/CI must set `SECURE_SSL_REDIRECT`, `SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE`, and `CSRF_COOKIE_SECURE` to `False` for plain HTTP tests.

## Models and business logic

- Keep heavy IO (Drive, n8n, external HTTP) in `app/services.py`; views stay thin.
- Use existing `TextChoices` on models (e.g. `User.Role`, `DoctorProfile.VerificationStatus`) instead of raw strings.
- New migrations: run `makemigrations` / review SQL impact; never edit applied migration files without explicit approval.

## API and auth

- Browser UI: session auth + CSRF. API: DRF viewsets with JWT (`simplejwt`) where existing patterns apply.
- Enforce role checks with `app.permissions` classes on viewsets/views; also filter querysets so users only see their own data (patients vs doctors vs admin).

## Example: role-gated permission

```python
from rest_framework.viewsets import ModelViewSet
from app.permissions import IsDoctorOrAdmin

class ExampleViewSet(ModelViewSet):
    permission_classes = [IsDoctorOrAdmin]

    def get_queryset(self):
        qs = super().get_queryset()
        if self.request.user.role == User.Role.DOCTOR:
            return qs.filter(doctor=self.request.user.doctorprofile)
        return qs
```

## Style

- Ruff: line length 120, Python 3.14 (`ruff.toml`). Fix import order (I) and undefined names (F) before commit.
